WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.400
When it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
as what to feed ourselves
and our families,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.760
we should rely not on
anecdotes, but on facts.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.080
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.160
Did you know that those who stay hydrated
tend to maintain a healthier body weight?

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.240
But is it cause and effect?
Here’s our first story.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.600
In my video series
starting with

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.400
"How to Get the Weight Loss
Benefits of Ephedra

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.920
Without the Risks,"

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.680
I noted how drinking water
affects the release

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.920
of the adrenal hormone
noradrenaline,

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.400
making it the safest metabolic
booster for weight loss,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.280
and recommended drinking
two cups (0.50 L) of cold water

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.120
on an empty stomach
a few times a day,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.400
ideally before meals,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.480
as a form of that
negative calorie preloading,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.660
as I discuss in my video
"Evidence-Based Weight Loss."

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.480
But what about just
staying hydrated in general?

00:00:58.480 --> 00:00:59.600
In a national survey,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.240
one of the weight control
practices most associated

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.840
with successful weight loss was
"Drink plenty of water."

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.280
But it was also associated

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.840
with unsuccessful attempts
at weight loss.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.080
It's just one of the most
popular weight loss tips

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.520
across the board,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.640
both in the mainstream media

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.400
and a common recommendation

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.280
given to patients
by their physicians.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.120
But does it work?

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.560
Can we just add water?

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.720
About a dozen studies have been
published on the matter,

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:27.280
and overall,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.720
there does appear to be
a weight-reducing benefit

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.160
to increased water consumption.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.360
What's the obvious confounder,
though?

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.360
Confounding factors, also
known as "lurking variables,"

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.360
are some third element that ends
up being the true explanation

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.520
for a supposed link
between two things.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.800
Here's a textbook example:

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.560
There may be a tight correlation

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.560
between ice cream sales
and drowning deaths,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.320
but that doesn't mean
ice cream causes drowning.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.560
A more likely explanation

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.800
is that there is
a lurking third variable—

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.040
like hot weather, summertime—

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.760
that explains
why drowning deaths are highest

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.720
when ice cream consumption
is at its peak.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.640
So, what might be
a confounding factor

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.880
that offers
an alternate explanation

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.120
of why those who drink more
tend to lose more weight?

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.800
Maybe it's because
those who drink more water

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.600
tend to drink less soda.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:22.680
So maybe the reason

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.100
more water is associated
to more weight loss

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.680
is because
there's a third variable,

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.000
a confounding variable,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.520
like less soda consumption
connected to both,

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.840
and that's the real cause.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.600
The primary reason
that the CDC, USDA,

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.800
American Medical Association,

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.520
American Diabetes Association,
American Heart Association,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.560
and American Academy
of Pediatrics

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.760
all recommend drinking water
for weight management

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.360
is as a replacement
of beverages with calories.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.120
Even replacing
one sugary beverage or beer

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.600
with water per day

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.320
is associated with a lower
incidence of obesity over time.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.080
American children and
adolescents drink so much soda

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.040
that replacing all
sugary beverages with water

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.520
could result in an average
reduction of 235 calories a day.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.360
And what about exercise?

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.240
That's another obvious
confounder candidate.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.360
After all,
who drinks lots of water?

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.840
Those who spend hours
working out.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.320
So, it's no wonder
water drinkers might be slimmer.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.400
A study
of dieting overweight women

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.240
took both soda and exercise
into account

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.560
and still, however,
found a benefit associated

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.240
with increased
water consumption.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.040
Over a year, those who drank
at least a liter of water a day

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.320
lost about five more pounds
(2.30 kg) on average

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.040
than those who didn't.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.600
The researchers were able
to control for physical activity

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.720
and intake of other beverages,
but what about other foods?

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.200
It turns out that
those who drink more water

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.400
also tend to eat
more fruits and vegetables,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.000
greens and beans,
and whole grains,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.760
and less total sugar,
and less fast food.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.000
No wonder
they're a healthier weight.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.840
To control for dietary factors,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.640
the scientific world had
to bring out the big guns,

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:00.360
Harvard's big cohort studies

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.480
that followed
the diets and health

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:05.080
of more than 100,000
doctors and nurses for decades.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.680
They were able to control
not just for other beverages

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.520
and lifestyle factors such as
exercise, smoking, sleeping,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.760
and TV watching,

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.800
but a wide range of healthy
and unhealthy food intakes

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:19.480
from fruits and vegetables
to meat and candy consumption.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.440
They were the first to show that

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.520
"increasing water intake per se

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.880
was independently
and significantly associated

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.120
with less weight gain"
over the long term.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.840
Consumption patterns
in these studies

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.560
were by self-report, though.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.120
Participants were just asked

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.880
to fill out detailed
questionnaires about their diet.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.280
For more
of an objective measure,

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.440
researchers directly assessed
people's hydration status

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.440
by assessing their blood
and urine concentrations.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:47.720
In both adults

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.080
and children,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.160
the more hydrated people were,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.800
the less likely they were
to be obese.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.640
Spot-checking urine from
nearly 10,000 men and women,

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.800
researchers found that
nearly half of obese individuals

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.080
were walking around
under-hydrated

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.480
compared to fewer than
one in three individuals

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.440
who were normal weight
or lighter.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.280
The problem with
snapshot-in-time studies

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.400
is that you don't know
which came first.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.560
Did under-hydration
lead to obesity,

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.000
or did obesity
lead to under-hydration?

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.200
At a heavier weight,
you actually need more water.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.920
The daily water requirements

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.840
of a man of average height
weighing 210 pounds (95 kg)

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.960
may actually be four cups
(0.95 L) more

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.040
than the same man
at 160 (73 kg) pounds.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.560
And who's more hydrated?

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.400
Those who eat
more water-rich foods

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.680
like fruits and vegetables.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.840
There's that specter
of confounding again.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.040
The only way to prove
cause-and-effect

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.920
is to put it to the test
in an interventional trial,

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.200
which I'll cover next.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.360
If you randomize
overweight adolescents

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.240
into one of two groups,

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.600
in which they're either advised
to drink eight cups (1.90 L)

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.640
of water a day or not,

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.600
what happens after six months?

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.680
The first question
you always have to ask

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.120
with interventional studies is:

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.960
Did the study participants
actually comply?

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.880
Both groups started out drinking
around two cups (0.50 L) a day,

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.560
and so the study
was designed to see

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:18.000
if there was a weight loss
benefit of consuming

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.280
six extra cups (1.45 L)
of water.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.080
Unfortunately, the difference in
water intake between the groups

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.520
came out to be less than
a cup and a half (0.35 L),

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.960
which wasn't enough
to show any benefit.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.360
Only one or two teens
in the water group

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.040
reportedly reached
the target intake.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.160
To improve compliance,

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:40.760
another set of researchers
asked kids to check their pee.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.560
The group of overweight
9- to 12-year-olds

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.120
randomized to
the water intervention

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.160
were told to increase
their water intake

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.920
to the point their urine became
straw-colored (a pale yellow).

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:52.680
Still, not every kid complied,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.960
but those who did
lost significantly more weight.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:59.640
Inspired by these small pilot
studies and early successes

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.560
with school-based interventions
in Europe,

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.560
researchers launched
the most ambitious study yet,

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.560
involving more
than a million students

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.800
in New York City public schools.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.640
They compared obesity rates
and weight gain

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:15.160
in schools that implemented

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.280
cooled, fast,
filtered water dispensers

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.960
compared to control schools
that hadn't,

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.280
and the increased water access
appeared to translate

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:28.160
into less weight gain and
lower rates of overweight kids.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:28.920
In the very least,

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:32.320
we should stop prohibiting water
from classrooms.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.400
The accompanying editorial in
the AMA's pediatrics journal

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:38.440
was entitled: "The Power
of a Simple Intervention

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:42.120
to Improve Student Health:
Just Add Water."

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.080
Couldn't that result
just be explained

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:47.520
by decreased consumption
of soft drinks?

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.880
The study was performed
a decade after

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:53.200
New York City schools
had already removed soda

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.600
from all the vending machines.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:58.680
They still sold
low-fat milk, though,

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:00.800
and that's what
the researchers suspect

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:02.840
may account for their findingsâ€”

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.080
the 12 percent drop
in milk purchases

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.800
after the water machines
were installed.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:11.120
Milk intake, like soda,
results in weight gain.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:12.560
This is true for skim milk

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.480
or even just
straight dairy protein,

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:16.840
pure whey or casein beverages

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.800
without the sugars
naturally occurring in milk.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.640
The increased fat mass
from drinking milk

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.160
may in part be from
the increased insulin secretion

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:28.200
caused by milk protein.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.440
Even dairy industry-funded
studies have found

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.160
that drinking less
than a cup of milk (0.25 L)

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:34.800
with a low glycemic index meal

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.680
can exaggerate the insulin spike

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:40.840
as much as if you had just eaten
high glycemic white bread.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.800
While milk can impair
fat burning,

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.280
water may have
the opposite effect.

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:49.920
To get to the bottom of the
water and weight loss question,

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.720
tightly controlled metabolic
experiments were performed

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.400
in which whole-body
protein and fat breakdown

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:58.440
were measured under
different degrees of hydration.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.120
Well-hydrated individuals

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.680
experienced
the best of both worlds,

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:06.880
increased fat burning
and decreased protein breakdown.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:09.440
The way the body responds
to high water intake

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:12.440
is similar to how it responds
to acute fasting,

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.040
a switching towards
fat as a fuel source

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.600
while trying
to spare the muscle.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:19.920
These were proof-of-principle
experiments, however,

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.520
with limited
real-world relevance,

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:24.640
as the high fluid states
were induced

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.720
not only by having
the participants drink

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:29.040
10 cups (2.5 L) of water
over a 12-hour period,

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.200
but also by dripping
extra free water

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:32.280
straight into their veins,

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.920
and even giving them
an antidiuretic hormone

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:37.680
to cause them to retain
even more water.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:40.360
There are, however,
mechanisms by which

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:45.720
our day-to-day hydration status
can affect our metabolism.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.320
When we get dehydrated,

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:50.440
our blood volume
actually shrinks.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:52.640
This drop is
detected by our kidneys

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:54.680
that release an enzyme
into our bloodstream

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:58.000
that triggers the cascade
that results in the formation

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.720
of a hormone called angiotensin.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.320
Angiotensin causes us
to become thirsty

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:05.160
and constricts our blood vessels

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.400
to compensate
for the diminished blood volume.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:09.920
(That's actually
how a popular class

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.560
of blood pressure-lowering
medications work.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:15.720
The ACE in "ACE inhibitors"
like captopril

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.840
stands for
Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme.)

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.560
That's not all that
angiotensin does, though.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.840
Drip it on human fat cells
in a petri dish

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:26.640
and they start piling in
more fat.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:27.880
This may help explain why those

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:30.680
with higher angiotensin levels
in their bloodstream

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:31.680
tend to be heavier.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.880
The thought is that
those who don't drink enough

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:37.080
end up with chronically elevated
angiotensin levels,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.040
which can lead to weight gain.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:41.840
The most convincing evidence
comes from genetic studies

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:43.600
showing that
those born predisposed

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.640
to higher angiotensin levels

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:49.120
are significantly more likely
to become obese.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.520
We can keep our levels down
in the normal range, though,

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:56.040
by staying adequately hydrated.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.609
Though many may distrust
the safety of tap water,

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.240
bottled water may be
no safer, cleaner,

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:06.920
or higher in quality than water
straight out of the faucet.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:10.840
There are risks to drinking
too much water, though.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:12.800
Even healthy kidneys
may only be able to handle

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.120
about three cups (0.70 L)
of water an hour.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.800
Beyond that we risk washing the
electrolytes out of our brain,

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:22.680
with potentially
lethal consequences.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:24.960
Can you just drink
a sports drink instead?

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.040
No; in fact, there
was a high-profile case

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:29.920
of a high school athlete
who died after drinking

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:32.000
two gallons of Gatorade.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.240
Drinking too much of anything
can be dangerous.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.240
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.000
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.880
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:48.680
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:52.440
If you see any graphs, charts,
graphics, images, or studies mentioned here,

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:55.880
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:58.180
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:02.800
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:05.880
My latest major book,
“How Not to Age” is out.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.560
Check it out from your
local public library.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:08.880
Of course, all the proceeds
I receive of the sales

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.720
of all my books
go to charity.

